Algorithms To Learn","tags":["A.I.","Deep Learning","Machine Learning"],"date":"2018-08-14T23:46:37.121"}}},{"node":{"fields":{"slug":"/posts/why-all-the-excitement-about-ai/","tagSlugs":["/tags/a-i/","/tags/machine-learning/","/tags/deep-learning/"]},"frontmatter":{"title":"Why All The Excitement About Artificial Intelligence","tags":["A.I.","Machine Learning","Deep Learning"],"date":"2018-08-14T23:46:37.121"}}},{"node":{"fields":{"slug":"/posts/being-a-good-machine-learning-data-scientist/","tagSlugs":["/tags/a-i/","/tags/data-science/","/tags/machine-learning/"]},"frontmatter":{"title":"Being a Good Machine Learning Engineer/Data Scientist","tags":["A.I.","Data Science","Machine Learning"],"date":"2018-08-11T23:46:37.121"}}},{"node":{"fields":{"slug":"/posts/a-software-interview-challenge/","tagSlugs":["/tags/software-engineering/","/tags/interview-prep/"]},"frontmatter":{"title":"A Software Interview Challenge","tags":["Software Engineering","Interview Prep"],"date":"2017-12-24T23:46:37.121"}}},{"node":{"fields":{"slug":"/posts/review-of-sigdial-semdial-2017/","tagSlugs":["/tags/natural-language-processing/","/tags/a-i/","/tags/deep-learning/"]},"frontmatter":{"title":"Review of SIGDial/SemDial 2017","tags":["Natural Language Processing","A.I.","Deep Learning"],"date":"2017-09-04T23:46:37.121"}}},{"node":{"fields":{"slug":"/posts/a-new-multiturn-dialogue-dataset/","tagSlugs":["/tags/natural-language-processing/","/tags/dataset/","/tags/dialogue/"]},"frontmatter":{"title":"A New Multi-Turn, Multi-Domain, Task-Oriented Dialogue Dataset","tags":["Natural Language Processing","Dataset","Dialogue"],"date":"2017-07-03T23:46:37.121"}}}]},"markdownRemark":{"id":"/Users/mihaileric/Documents/Projects/mihail911.github.io/src/pages/articles/2019-28-04---Feedforward-Neural-Networks/index.md absPath of file >>> MarkdownRemark","html":"<figure>\n    \n  <a class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\" href=\"/static/neural_network_meme-2d98b614a083d918e3cf714c08493946-2d4eb.jpeg\" style=\"display: block\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\n  \n  <span class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\" style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 512px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\">\n    <span class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\" style=\"padding-bottom: 51.7578125%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url(&apos;data:image/jpeg;base64,&apos;); background-size: cover; display: block;\">\n      <img class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\" style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\" alt=\"neural network meme inception\" title=\"\" src=\"/static/neural_network_meme-2d98b614a083d918e3cf714c08493946-2d4eb.jpeg\" srcset=\"/static/neural_network_meme-2d98b614a083d918e3cf714c08493946-63500.jpeg 240w,\n/static/neural_network_meme-2d98b614a083d918e3cf714c08493946-7c5bd.jpeg 480w,\n/static/neural_network_meme-2d98b614a083d918e3cf714c08493946-2d4eb.jpeg 512w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 512px) 100vw, 512px\">\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    \n</figure>\n<p>In this post, we will take our first deep dive into deep learning. Because deep learning\nis primarily the study of neural networks, we will spend the next few posts exploring the ins-and-outs of various classes\nof neural network models that each have different architectures and use-cases.</p>\n<p>In this post, we will be studying\nthe most vanilla flavor of neural networks: <strong>feedforward neural networks</strong>. Consider this diagram of running an image\nthrough some neural network-based classifier to determine it’s an image of a dog:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/basic_neural_network-54bd4d259b8ad436765b8fec165e1952-691ce.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 900px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 36.888888888888886%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Basic neural network diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/basic_neural_network-54bd4d259b8ad436765b8fec165e1952-691ce.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/basic_neural_network-54bd4d259b8ad436765b8fec165e1952-36d23.png 240w,\n/static/basic_neural_network-54bd4d259b8ad436765b8fec165e1952-1d6be.png 480w,\n/static/basic_neural_network-54bd4d259b8ad436765b8fec165e1952-691ce.png 900w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 900px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>The network in the middle is the main engine doing our classifying, and it turns out this is our first example of a\nfeedforward neural network. So what’s going on here? Let’s dive in and see!</p>\n<h2>Building Up the Network</h2>\n<p>Let’s zoom in on just the model from our image:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/zoomInNetworkDiagram-67568cc7f04eb0a22b50838ce62d5f45-ccd77.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 342px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 77.19298245614036%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Zoom in on neural network diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/zoomInNetworkDiagram-67568cc7f04eb0a22b50838ce62d5f45-ccd77.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/zoomInNetworkDiagram-67568cc7f04eb0a22b50838ce62d5f45-77586.png 240w,\n/static/zoomInNetworkDiagram-67568cc7f04eb0a22b50838ce62d5f45-ccd77.png 342w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 342px) 100vw, 342px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>We can label each component of this feedforward network as follows:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/nnetLayerLabelDiagram-445d884e3a3618ec9c961e1db38f758c-5a1a4.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 406px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 110.34482758620689%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Neural network layer label diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/nnetLayerLabelDiagram-445d884e3a3618ec9c961e1db38f758c-5a1a4.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/nnetLayerLabelDiagram-445d884e3a3618ec9c961e1db38f758c-ede8c.png 240w,\n/static/nnetLayerLabelDiagram-445d884e3a3618ec9c961e1db38f758c-5a1a4.png 406w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 406px) 100vw, 406px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>It turns out this network is performing certain layer-based\ncomputations. To understand what each layer is doing, we must first begin at the start of the model computation,\nwhich is where we provide the input data.</p>\n<p>Recall that we can represent each point in a dataset through some featurized representation, and this is typically where we start\nwhen providing the point to a machine learning model. The exact features we extract often depend on the\nproblem domain.</p>\n<p>In the case of image classification, a common representation of an image is as a 3-d matrix (also known as a <em>tensor</em>)\nof <span style=\"color:red\">red</span>/<span style=\"color:green\">green</span>/<span style=\"color:blue\">blue</span> pixel values.\nTherefore if our image is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.64444em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:0.64444em;vertical-align:0em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>x<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.64444em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:0.64444em;vertical-align:0em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span> pixels, each pixel would\nconsist of 3 values, one representing the strength of each of the red, green, and blue color channels. Our image\nrepresented as a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.64444em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:0.64444em;vertical-align:0em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>x<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.64444em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:0.64444em;vertical-align:0em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>x<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.64444em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:0.64444em;vertical-align:0em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span></span> grid of values would look as follows:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/imagePixelReprDiagram-3c429f9988efc3d887a2250e9c114d04-cbd27.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 960px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 51.9455252918288%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Image red green blue pixel value representation\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/imagePixelReprDiagram-3c429f9988efc3d887a2250e9c114d04-3d61e.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/imagePixelReprDiagram-3c429f9988efc3d887a2250e9c114d04-d182c.png 240w,\n/static/imagePixelReprDiagram-3c429f9988efc3d887a2250e9c114d04-5220f.png 480w,\n/static/imagePixelReprDiagram-3c429f9988efc3d887a2250e9c114d04-3d61e.png 960w,\n/static/imagePixelReprDiagram-3c429f9988efc3d887a2250e9c114d04-cbd27.png 1028w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 960px) 100vw, 960px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>We can further collapse this 3-d representation into a single vector of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\cdot 100\\cdot 3 = 30,000</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.64444em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:0.8388800000000001em;vertical-align:-0.19444em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span> values. We do this\nmerely for convenience, without modifying any of the underlying information:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/collapsedVectorDiagram-816054103b91b159677e84b4c5ed2bdf-0ed47.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 960px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 31.714719271623675%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Collapsed image vector representation\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/collapsedVectorDiagram-816054103b91b159677e84b4c5ed2bdf-3d61e.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/collapsedVectorDiagram-816054103b91b159677e84b4c5ed2bdf-d182c.png 240w,\n/static/collapsedVectorDiagram-816054103b91b159677e84b4c5ed2bdf-5220f.png 480w,\n/static/collapsedVectorDiagram-816054103b91b159677e84b4c5ed2bdf-3d61e.png 960w,\n/static/collapsedVectorDiagram-816054103b91b159677e84b4c5ed2bdf-0ed47.png 1318w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 960px) 100vw, 960px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>For the sake of our example, rather than operating on a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100,000</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.64444em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:0.8388800000000001em;vertical-align:-0.19444em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>-d vector, imagine that we use a smaller representation of the picture consisting of a 3-d vector: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>4</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V = (1, 0.4, 2)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.75em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord mathit\" style=\"margin-right:0.22222em;\">V</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>. Here we have picked these values arbitrarily.</p>\n<p>Now let’s take a 3-d weight vector <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W = (-1, 0.2, 1)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.75em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord mathit\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">W</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> and compute the sum <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_1 \\cdot V_1 + W_2 \\cdot V_2 + W_3 \\cdot V_3 = -1 \\cdot 1 + 0.2 \\cdot 0.4 + 1 \\cdot 2 = 1.08</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.68333em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:0.83333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathit\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">W</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.30110799999999993em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5500000000000003em;margin-left:-0.13889em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathit\" style=\"margin-right:0.22222em;\">V</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.30110799999999993em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5500000000000003em;margin-left:-0.22222em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathit\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">W</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.30110799999999993em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5500000000000003em;margin-left:-0.13889em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathit\" style=\"margin-right:0.22222em;\">V</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.30110799999999993em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5500000000000003em;margin-left:-0.22222em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathit\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">W</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.30110799999999993em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5500000000000003em;margin-left:-0.13889em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathit\" style=\"margin-right:0.22222em;\">V</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.30110799999999993em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5500000000000003em;margin-left:-0.22222em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222222222222222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">8</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>Now let’s further feed that sum through a sigmoid function:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/sigmoidApplicationDiagram-c5c752856efb4a53dba43753949a67ac-8cb32.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 552px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 27.536231884057973%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Feeding through sigmoid function diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/sigmoidApplicationDiagram-c5c752856efb4a53dba43753949a67ac-8cb32.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/sigmoidApplicationDiagram-c5c752856efb4a53dba43753949a67ac-ff5ee.png 240w,\n/static/sigmoidApplicationDiagram-c5c752856efb4a53dba43753949a67ac-7dc04.png 480w,\n/static/sigmoidApplicationDiagram-c5c752856efb4a53dba43753949a67ac-8cb32.png 552w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 552px) 100vw, 552px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>We then set that result as the value of the circle in the first layer:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/firstUnitDiagram-f746a28104750239c5e5948e1df8c358-8ae82.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 322px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 107.45341614906832%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"First unit neural network diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/firstUnitDiagram-f746a28104750239c5e5948e1df8c358-8ae82.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/firstUnitDiagram-f746a28104750239c5e5948e1df8c358-ddff8.png 240w,\n/static/firstUnitDiagram-f746a28104750239c5e5948e1df8c358-8ae82.png 322w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 322px) 100vw, 322px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>This result is our first example of a <strong>neuron</strong> in the neural network terminology. Because the term <strong>neuron</strong> is a\ngross abuse of the biological term (and we don’t want to annoy any of our neuroscience friends), let’s just call it a <strong>compute unit</strong> for\nour purposes.</p>\n<p>Now let’s use a new weight vector <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_2 = (3, 0.3, -0.1)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.75em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathit\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">W</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.30110799999999993em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5500000000000003em;margin-left:-0.13889em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.2777777777777778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> and perform the same computation with our feature vector, namely\ntaking a linear sum and feeding it through a sigmoid function. If we repeat this computation with the new weight vector, we should get the result <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>8</mn><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.81</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.64444em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:0.64444em;vertical-align:0em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span>,\nwhich becomes the value of the second compute unit in our new layer:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/secondUnitDiagram-d258227fa9d3b2f8d4a4cf3f4a6643fb-ea9d9.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 328px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 107.31707317073172%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Second unit neural network diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/secondUnitDiagram-d258227fa9d3b2f8d4a4cf3f4a6643fb-ea9d9.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/secondUnitDiagram-d258227fa9d3b2f8d4a4cf3f4a6643fb-5ad95.png 240w,\n/static/secondUnitDiagram-d258227fa9d3b2f8d4a4cf3f4a6643fb-ea9d9.png 328w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 328px) 100vw, 328px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>We can repeat this computation for as many compute units as we want. The important thing is to use a different weight vector for each compute\nunit.</p>\n<p>Let’s say we repeat this computation for 3 units in total with some weight vectors getting the following values:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/thirdUnitDiagram-a76d16fe0c48091409b5cabba04f64c5-0f05a.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 362px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 119.33701657458565%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Third unit neural network diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/thirdUnitDiagram-a76d16fe0c48091409b5cabba04f64c5-0f05a.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/thirdUnitDiagram-a76d16fe0c48091409b5cabba04f64c5-9b33e.png 240w,\n/static/thirdUnitDiagram-a76d16fe0c48091409b5cabba04f64c5-0f05a.png 362w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 362px) 100vw, 362px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>Congratulations! These computed values form the first layer of our neural network! These intermediate layers of a neural network after the layer where the inputs\nare fed in are called <strong>hidden layers</strong>.</p>\n<p>This particular layer of computations also has a special name: a <strong>fully connected layer</strong>. Again we can have as many compute units as we want\nin the layer, and that number is something we can tune so as to maximize performance.</p>\n<p>In addition, the sigmoid function we chose to\nrun our linear sum through has a special name in the neural network community: an <strong>activation function</strong>. It turns out we can\neasily choose different activation functions to feed our linear sum through. We will explore a few commonly used variants in the next post.</p>\n<p>One last note:\nwe didn’t really mention how we chose the values of our weight vectors. This seems like an important detail to mention because the weights\ndetermine how certain features get upweighted (or downweighted) at a given compute unit. The truth is we picked them randomly, and\nthis is basically what is done in practice (with a few caveats which we will discuss later).</p>\n<p>Now that we have this vector of 3 compute units at the\nfirst layer of our network, why not repeat the same set of operations as before?</p>\n<p>So that’s what we do. We pick new weights per compute unit, take\na linear sum with the vector of values from the first layer, and run the result through a sigmoid function. This gives us the following:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/secondLayerDiagram-e668e6cdfbe4a550ea1eec1fc362e2bb-199ec.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 516px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 85.65891472868216%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Second layer neural network diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/secondLayerDiagram-e668e6cdfbe4a550ea1eec1fc362e2bb-199ec.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/secondLayerDiagram-e668e6cdfbe4a550ea1eec1fc362e2bb-3e79f.png 240w,\n/static/secondLayerDiagram-e668e6cdfbe4a550ea1eec1fc362e2bb-00356.png 480w,\n/static/secondLayerDiagram-e668e6cdfbe4a550ea1eec1fc362e2bb-199ec.png 516w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 516px) 100vw, 516px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>Nice! This is the second layer of our network! Let’s assume that we are doing a 3-way classification task where we are trying to predict whether\nan image is of a <strong>cat</strong>, a <strong>dog</strong>, or <strong>neither</strong>.</p>\n<p>Therefore we want to end up with three values that give us the probability for the values <strong>cat</strong>, <strong>dog</strong>,\nand <strong>neither</strong>. Let’s run our second layer through another similar set of computations. This will give us a distribution over three values as follows:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/thirdLayerDiagram-de27cf08cb047d788b3618a8e9cd1786-f9281.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 654px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 63.30275229357799%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Third layer neural network diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/thirdLayerDiagram-de27cf08cb047d788b3618a8e9cd1786-f9281.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/thirdLayerDiagram-de27cf08cb047d788b3618a8e9cd1786-d33b4.png 240w,\n/static/thirdLayerDiagram-de27cf08cb047d788b3618a8e9cd1786-011a1.png 480w,\n/static/thirdLayerDiagram-de27cf08cb047d788b3618a8e9cd1786-f9281.png 654w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 654px) 100vw, 654px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>This set of computations through the layers of our network is called the forward pass. Now we finally see why these are called feedforward\nnetworks: we are <strong>feeding</strong> values <strong>forward</strong> through the network.</p>\n<p>One important note on hidden layers and compute units per layer of a neural network. In general, the number of layers\nand the number of units are both hyperparameters that we can adjust as necessary to achieve the best generalization error in our network.</p>\n<h2>Backpropagating Errors</h2>\n<p>These computations are all nice and good if our network\npredicts the right value for a given input. However, it seems pretty unlikely that if our weights were randomly chosen that our model would know to predict\nthe right value.</p>\n<p>So what’s missing? It turns out our feedforward pass is only half the story. We have said nothing about how to actually train the model\nusing our data. Let’s assume that during our forward pass our neural network made a mistake and instead predicted a <strong>cat</strong>:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/networkMistakeDiagram-09d47838d5282b3bde444185dfb5c8d6-ea2b1.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 914px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 40.70021881838075%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Neural network incorrect classification diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/networkMistakeDiagram-09d47838d5282b3bde444185dfb5c8d6-ea2b1.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/networkMistakeDiagram-09d47838d5282b3bde444185dfb5c8d6-bd51e.png 240w,\n/static/networkMistakeDiagram-09d47838d5282b3bde444185dfb5c8d6-f74f8.png 480w,\n/static/networkMistakeDiagram-09d47838d5282b3bde444185dfb5c8d6-ea2b1.png 914w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 914px) 100vw, 914px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>It turns out that though our network made a mistake, we can still compute a cost for the values that our network computed. Intuitively it seems\nthat if our network predicted <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.35, 0.45, 0.2)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.75em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> for <strong>(dog, cat, neither)</strong> that is <em>less wrong</em> than if it had predicted <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>8</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.1, 0.8, 0.1)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.75em;\"></span><span class=\"strut bottom\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mord rule\" style=\"margin-right:0.16666666666666666em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mord\">.</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>So we can formally associate a value for this level of <em>wrongness</em> using a cost function, similar to what we did previously with models such as linear regression. We will discuss some details about the possible cost functions we can use for different tasks in later posts.</p>\n<p>For now let’s assume the cost calculated is 5. Neural networks as we have defined have a special property called <strong>differentiability</strong>, which\nmeans we can compute derivatives of the cost function with respect to all the weights. Woah, that was a mouthful.</p>\n<p>We won’t\ngo into the nitty-gritty details, but let’s take the weights of the the first compute unit of the final layer (of the neural network in our running example). We can compute gradients indicating how much we should update these weights to get closer to predicting the right value (thereby lowering the value of the cost function).</p>\n<p>Below, the gradient of the first unit with respect to the weights in the preceding layer flow backwards. This is indicated by the red arrows:</p>\n<p>\n  <a\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-link\"\n    href=\"/static/backflowDiagram-6954f134aa997e95135ca44ce8c1346d-be599.png\"\n    style=\"display: block\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener\"\n  >\n  \n  <span\n    class=\"gatsby-resp-image-wrapper\"\n    style=\"position: relative; display: block; ; max-width: 648px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\"\n  >\n    <span\n      class=\"gatsby-resp-image-background-image\"\n      style=\"padding-bottom: 64.81481481481481%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"\n    >\n      <img\n        class=\"gatsby-resp-image-image\"\n        style=\"width: 100%; height: 100%; margin: 0; vertical-align: middle; position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; box-shadow: inset 0px 0px 0px 400px white;\"\n        alt=\"Back flow neural network gradient diagram\"\n        title=\"\"\n        src=\"/static/backflowDiagram-6954f134aa997e95135ca44ce8c1346d-be599.png\"\n        srcset=\"/static/backflowDiagram-6954f134aa997e95135ca44ce8c1346d-06bae.png 240w,\n/static/backflowDiagram-6954f134aa997e95135ca44ce8c1346d-9f697.png 480w,\n/static/backflowDiagram-6954f134aa997e95135ca44ce8c1346d-be599.png 648w\"\n        sizes=\"(max-width: 648px) 100vw, 648px\"\n      />\n    </span>\n  </span>\n  \n  </a>\n    </p>\n<p>Once we have these gradients, we update the weights as necessary. Notice how here the end result is being used to send information backward in the network.\nThis backward flow of signal is called <strong>backpropagation</strong>.</p>\n<p>It turns out we can compute these gradients for all the weights in a given layer.\nOnce we have computed the gradients and updated the weights in the last layer, we compute the derivatives and update the weights in the second layer.</p>\n<p>We can continue this all the way to the first layer. These backpropagation updates are\ndone using gradient descent. In practice, we update all the weights in the entire network for some number of iterations until our cost function decreases\nas much as we would like.</p>\n<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>\n<p>This concludes our (hopefully) gentle introduction to feedforward neural networks. While they may be the most vanilla\nnetworks, in practice feedforward networks can be successfully applied on many regression and classification tasks. Though we used the example of image classification,\nwe can apply feedforward networks to many other problem domains.</p>\n<p>In general, the compute units in neural network hidden\nlayers learn many powerful representations of the data during training.\nIn the next lessons we will be building out some of the details of neural network theory.</p>\n<p><em>Shameless Pitch Alert: If you’re interested in practicing MLOps, data science, and data engineering concepts, check out <a href=\"https://www.confetti.ai/?utm_source=personal-blog&#x26;utm_medium=web&#x26;utm_campaign=deep-dive-into-neural-networks\">Confetti AI</a> the premier educational machine learning platform used by students at Harvard, Stanford, Berkeley, and more!</em></p>","fields":{"tagSlugs":["/tags/machine-learning/","/tags/a-i/","/tags/deep-learning/"]},"frontmatter":{"title":"Deep Dive Into Neural Networks","tags":["Machine Learning","A.I.","Deep Learning"],"date":"2019-04-28T23:46:37.121","description":"We begin a deep dive into deep learning by investigating feedforward neural networks."}}},"pathContext":{"slug":"/posts/deep-dive-into-neural-networks/"}}


